Trust this connection's own just-committed refs in chunkStoreForceRefresh
(Emscripten only).

chunkStoreForceRefresh's fast path (csDiskStateMatchesMemory) proves the
on-disk store still matches memory by stat-ing the file and reading its tail
root record through the chunk store's own long-lived file handle. On native
VFSes a write made through that handle is immediately visible to a stat on
the same handle, so the fast path reliably fires right after this
connection's own commit -- and just as reliably still catches a concurrent
peer writer, since a peer's write changes the physical file size that stat
reports.

Emscripten's DriveFS (used by the wasm build to share a filesystem across
worker threads) does not have the first property: writes are buffered on the
open stream and only pushed back to the backing store when the stream is
closed, but stat/getattr calls are served from the backing store regardless
of any still-open writer. So on wasm, csDiskStateMatchesMemory's stat-based
check reports a same-connection commit as stale even though nothing actually
changed, which sends chunkStoreForceRefresh down the reload path. Right
after `dolt_clone`, that reload replaces the in-memory refs table with a
fresh (but, per the DriveFS quirk above, momentarily still-stale) view that
is missing the `origin` remote `dolt_clone` itself just added, so the very
next statement -- `dolt_remote('set-url', 'origin', ...)`, run by
rusqdoltlite to restore the canonical clone URL -- fails with
SQLITE_NOTFOUND ("remote not found").

Add a one-shot ChunkStore::bTrustLocalAfterCommit flag, compiled in only
under __EMSCRIPTEN__: chunkStoreCommit sets it after a successful on-disk
commit, and chunkStoreForceRefresh's fast path treats it as equivalent to
csDiskStateMatchesMemory returning true, clearing it either way once
consumed. This is guarded to Emscripten specifically because it trades away
disk-verified peer-write detection for that one call -- safe there because
Emscripten's execution model is synchronous and single-threaded, so nothing
can interleave a conflicting write between this connection's own commit and
its very next statement, but not a trade worth making on native, where the
original disk probe already works correctly and cheaply catches a real
concurrent writer. Native and other non-Emscripten targets compile
byte-for-byte identically to before this patch.

diff --git a/doltlite.c b/doltlite.c
index 360f935..a80a610 100644
--- a/doltlite.c
+++ b/doltlite.c
@@ -23646,6 +23646,25 @@ struct ChunkStore {
 
   /* Prevent checkpoint reentry through VFS write hooks. */
   int checkpointActive;
+
+#if defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
+  /* Set right after this connection's own chunkStoreCommit persists refs to
+  ** disk; consumed (cleared) by the very next chunkStoreForceRefresh call.
+  ** Emscripten's DriveFS (used by the wasm build) only makes a written
+  ** file's stat/size visible to callers after the writing stream is
+  ** closed, so csDiskStateMatchesMemory's disk probe can report a
+  ** same-connection commit as stale even though nothing changed. This flag
+  ** lets the immediate post-commit refresh trust the state it just wrote
+  ** instead of tripping the reload path, avoiding a spurious "remote not
+  ** found" warning from statements like dolt_remote('set-url') that
+  ** immediately follow a clone/pull on the same connection. Emscripten's
+  ** execution model is synchronous and single-threaded, so there is no
+  ** point between this connection's own commit and its very next statement
+  ** where a peer could interleave a conflicting write -- unlike native,
+  ** where trusting local state here would blind chunkStoreForceRefresh to
+  ** a real concurrent writer, so this stays off outside Emscripten. */
+  u8 bTrustLocalAfterCommit;
+#endif
 };
 
 void csManifestSeal(u8 *aBuf);
@@ -97405,6 +97424,25 @@ struct ChunkStore {
 
   /* Prevent checkpoint reentry through VFS write hooks. */
   int checkpointActive;
+
+#if defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
+  /* Set right after this connection's own chunkStoreCommit persists refs to
+  ** disk; consumed (cleared) by the very next chunkStoreForceRefresh call.
+  ** Emscripten's DriveFS (used by the wasm build) only makes a written
+  ** file's stat/size visible to callers after the writing stream is
+  ** closed, so csDiskStateMatchesMemory's disk probe can report a
+  ** same-connection commit as stale even though nothing changed. This flag
+  ** lets the immediate post-commit refresh trust the state it just wrote
+  ** instead of tripping the reload path, avoiding a spurious "remote not
+  ** found" warning from statements like dolt_remote('set-url') that
+  ** immediately follow a clone/pull on the same connection. Emscripten's
+  ** execution model is synchronous and single-threaded, so there is no
+  ** point between this connection's own commit and its very next statement
+  ** where a peer could interleave a conflicting write -- unlike native,
+  ** where trusting local state here would blind chunkStoreForceRefresh to
+  ** a real concurrent writer, so this stays off outside Emscripten. */
+  u8 bTrustLocalAfterCommit;
+#endif
 };
 
 void csManifestSeal(u8 *aBuf);
@@ -277295,6 +277333,9 @@ int chunkStoreCommit(ChunkStore *cs){
     acquiredLock = 1;
   }
   rc = csCommitToFile(cs);
+#if defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
+  if( rc==SQLITE_OK ) cs->bTrustLocalAfterCommit = 1;
+#endif
   if( acquiredLock ) chunkStoreUnlock(cs);
   return rc;
 }
@@ -277484,9 +277525,18 @@ int chunkStoreForceRefresh(ChunkStore *cs){
   ** matches this handle's state, the reload — which replays the entire WAL —
   ** is a no-op and can be skipped. Local uncommitted appends fall through so
   ** the reload path keeps reporting SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT. */
+#if defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
+  if( cs->staging.nRecentUncommitted==0
+   && (csDiskStateMatchesMemory(cs) || cs->bTrustLocalAfterCommit) ){
+    cs->bTrustLocalAfterCommit = 0;
+    return SQLITE_OK;
+  }
+  cs->bTrustLocalAfterCommit = 0;
+#else
   if( cs->staging.nRecentUncommitted==0 && csDiskStateMatchesMemory(cs) ){
     return SQLITE_OK;
   }
+#endif
   /* Reload even under a pinned snapshot — the VC write needs the true on-disk
   ** branch tips. Unpin around it (the heuristic refresh path suppresses reloads
   ** while pinned), then restore the pin. */
